_high_45.raw\n20240124_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_TMTpro_high_46.raw\n20240124_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_TMTpro_high_47.raw\n20240124_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_TMTpro_high_48.raw\n20240124_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_TMTpro_high_49.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_01.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_02.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_03.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_04.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_05.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_06.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_07.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_08.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_09.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_10.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_11.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_12.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_13.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_14.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_15.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_16.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_17.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_18.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_19.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_20.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_21.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_22.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_23.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_24.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_25.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_26.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_27.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_28.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_29.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_30.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_31.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_32.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_33.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_34.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_35.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_36.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_37.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_38.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_39.raw\n20240208_RH28_COREAD_PhoX_5k_40.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nPhoXplex: Combining phospho-enrichable crosslinking with isobaric labelling for quantitative proteome-wide mapping of protein interfaces\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIntegrating crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) into structural biology workflows provides valuable information about the spatial arrangement of amino acid stretches, which can guide elucidation of protein assembly architecture. Additionally, combination of XL-MS with peptide quantitation techniques is a powerful approach to delineate protein interface dynamics across diverse conditions. While XL-MS is increasingly effective with isolated proteins or small complexes, its application to whole-cell samples poses technical challenges related to analysis depth and throughput. The use of enrichable crosslinkers has greatly improved the detectability of protein interfaces in a proteome-wide scale, facilitating global protein-protein interaction mapping. Therefore, bringing together enrichable crosslinking and multiplexed peptide quantification is an appealing approach to enable comparative characterization of structural attributes of proteins and protein interactions. Here, we combined phospho-enrichable crosslinking with TMT labelling to develop a streamline workflow (PhoXplex) for the detection of differential structural features across a panel of cell lines in a global scale. We achieved deep coverage with quantification of over 9000 crosslinking events including potentially novel interactions. Overlaying AlphaFold predictions and disorder protein annotations enables exploration of the quantitative crosslinking dataset, to reveal possible associations between mutations and protein structures. Lastly, we discuss current shortcomings and perspectives for deep whole-cell profiling of protein interfaces at large-scale.\n\n[METHODS]\nCell pellets were lysed in 400 μL of 20 mM HEPES, 150 mM NaCl, pH=7.5 buffer supplemented with 4 μL Halt Protease Inhibitor Cocktail (Thermo, #78429) and 0.5 μL universal nuclease (Pierce, #88701) with 10 sec probe sonication at a pulse of 1 sec every 3 seconds at amplitude 30% (EpiShear) on ice. Bradford assay was performed to normalise protein amount to 0.7 mg for every cell line and each sample was diluted up to 490 μL with the lysis buffer (~1.4 μg/μL). A 50 mM crosslinker stock solution was prepared by dissolving 5.5 mg of TBDSPP (Thermo, A52287) in 200 μL of extra dry acetonitrile. TBDSPP stock solution was added in each cell lysate at a final concentration of 1 mM (10 μL). Samples were incubated at room temperature for 1 hour and the reaction was terminated by adding 1 M ammonium bicarbonate to a final concentration of 20 mM, followed by incubation for 15 mins before adding 5 μL of alkaline phosphatase (Pierce, #31392), and incubating in a thermomixer at 37°C at 500 rpm overnight for protein dephosphorylation. Cysteines were reduced with tris-2-carboxyethyl phosphine (TCEP) at a final concentration of 5 mM and alkylated with freshly prepared iodoacetamide at a final concentration of 10 mM, simultaneously at room temperature for 1 hour. To remove the excess of the crosslinker, proteins were precipitated with the addition of 100 μL of 10 M of trichloroacetic acid (TCA), vortex and centrifugation at 4°C for 10 mins at 14680 rpm. Following with acetone, supernatant was discarded and 200 μL of 100 mM TEAB buffer was added. For digestion of the protein pellets, trypsin solution of 500 ng/μL (Pierce #90058) was added in each sample at a final concentration of 50 ng/μL and samples were incubated at 37°C with shaking at 900 rpm for 16 hours. To deprotect the crosslinker prior to phospho-enrichment, 6.6 μL of 100% TFA was added (3%) and incubated for 1 hour at 37°C in a thermomixer set at 500 rpm. Phosphopeptide enrichment was performed using the High-select Fe-NTA phosphopeptide enrichment kit according to manufacturer’s protocol (Thermo, A32992). Enriched samples were reconstituted in 25 μL of 100 mM TEAB and labelled with 10 μL of TMTpro-18plex reagents dissolved in extra dry acetonitrile (TMTpro: 25 μg/μL, Thermo). Offline peptide fractionation was based on high-pH reversed phase (RP) chromatography using the Waters XBridge C18 column (2.1 × 150 mm, 3.5 μm) on a Dionex UltiMate 3000 HPLC system at a flow rate of 0.2 mL/min. Part of the high-pH fractions was analysed with LC-MS for identification of crosslinked peptides and quantification of proteins using the linear peptides. The remaining sample solutions were pooled again and subjected to ultrafiltration (3kDa and 5kDa MWCO) followed by high-pH reversed phase fractionation of the high MW part. The analysis of peptides was performed by online nanoLC-MS/MS on an Orbitrap Ascend Tribrid mass spectrometer coupled with an UltiMate 3000 RSLCnano System (Thermo). Samples were first loaded on a trap column (100 μm id × 2 cm, PepMap C18, 5 μm, 100Å) at 10 μL/min with 0.1% TFA loading buffer for 3 min and then separated on an analytical column (Waters, nanoE MZ PST BEH130 C18, 1.7 μm, 75 μm × 250 mm) over an 80 min linear gradient of 3-29% of 80% CH3CN/0.1% formic acid at 300 nL/min. The Orbitrap Ascend was operated in a data-dependent acquisition top speed mode (3 sec) targeting peptide precursors with charge states +3 to +8. MS scans were acquired with a resolution of 120000 within a m/z range of 380-1400. MS2 spectra were acquired with HCD fragmentation (higher collision dissociation) with collision energy 36% and a quadrupole isolation width of 0.7 Th (Thomson unit), detection in the Orbitrap with 45000 resolution, 200% normalized AGC, and maximum injection time of 91 ms. Targeted precursors were dynamically excluded from further isolation and activation for 45 secs with 10 ppm mass tolerance. Raw LC-MS files were processed with pLink version 2.3.11 for identification of cross-linked, loop-linked, mono-linked and regular peptides. Precursor and fragment mass tolerances were 20 ppm and 0.02 Da respectively. The linker mass of PhoX (+209.972 Da) with a mono mass of +227.982 Da (PhoX hydrolysed) targeting Lysine and protein n-terminus were configured and used for the search. Additionally, Carbamidomethyl at C (+57.021464 Da) and TMTpro (+304.207146 Da) at peptide n-terminus were selected as fixed modifications, as well as TMTpro (+304.207146 Da) at K as variable modification. Spectra were searched against a fasta file containing 9405 UniProt entries comprising the COREAD cell line proteome as previously determined, for tryptic peptides with up to 3 missed-cleavages and minimum peptide length 5. Crosslinks were filtered at PSM level FDR<1% separately for intra- and inter-links with 20 ppm tolerance.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
